JOURNAL OF THE AMERICAN HELICOPTER SOCIETY 63, 022006 (2018)
Analysis of Dynamic Stall Mechanisms in Helicopter Rotor Environment
Fran ̧cois Richez∗ Research Scientist ONERA—The French Aerospace Lab Meudon, France
A helicopter reaches its flight envelope limit at high-thrust forward flight or maneuver with high load factors because of dynamic stall. This phenomenon is due to complex unsteady three-dimensional flow separation mechanisms that occur on the retreating blade and can lead to structural damage of the pitch links. Because of the complexity of the flow conditions, a detailed description of dynamic stall in helicopter environment is still lacking. This paper proposes to investigate numerically an isolated 7A wind tunnel scaled rotor in a high-thrust forward flight. The objective is to make use of this numerical simulation to identify the mechanisms that lead to stall in this rotor configuration. Despite the high level of complexity of this flow, the comparison with experiment in terms of section normal forces and pitching moments is in very satisfactory agreement. The numerical results indicate three different separated flow regions, each one of them being characterized by different mechanisms. Furthermore, a blade–vortex interaction process is observed as a triggering factor of dynamic stall.
