angle of attack of the retreating blade. Thus, very high angles of attack can be reached on a large portion of the retreating blade side of the rotor disk, leading the boundary layer to massively separate from the blade suction side. This flow separation induces an increase of the drag, and as a consequence, of the fuel consumption. But most importantly, dynamic stall also generates large pitching moment oscillations of the blade that are responsible for large pitch link load variations that can lead to structural damage and the loss of pilot control. The flight envelope of helicopters is thus limited because of this dynamic stall phenomenon. The complexity of dynamic stall encountered on helicopter rotor configurations led many researchers to first consider simplified configurations such as two-dimensional airfoils at low-frequency pitching motions (Ref. 1). Piziali provided an extensive data base of a NACA0015 airfoil in dynamic stall conditions (Ref. 2) that has been widely used to assess the predictive capabilities of different turbulence models of CFD approaches (Refs. 3–6). More recently, several wind tunnel test campaigns were devoted, at ONERA and DLR, to an OA209 airfoil, providing particle image velocimetry and unsteady wall pressure measurements (Refs. 7 and 8). These experimental data were used to validate unsteady Reynolds-averaged Navier–Stokes (URANS) simulation tools. This twodimensional OA209 stall investigation allowed some requirements to be defined in terms of turbulence modeling and grid resolutions (Ref. 9). The influence of laminar–turbulent transition was numerically investigated in Refs. 10 and 11. An extensive analysis of the time resolution was proposed by Liggett and Smith for the case of VR7 airfoil in dynamic stall condition (Ref. 12). More recently, the configuration of a finite-span three-dimensional wing was numerically investigated by means of both URANS (Refs. 13–15) and zonal detached-eddy simulation (Ref. 16) approaches and validated with the experimental data of Ref. 8. The wing was composed of a single OA209 airfoil, with zero twist and an aspect ratio of 2.6. The numerical results showed a fairly good agreement with experiments and provided helpful insights into the dynamics of the flow separation around this oscillating three-dimensional wing. In Ref. 13, Costes et al. identified that stall first occurred in the inboard part of the blade. Then, a strong spanwise velocity appeared in the confined separated region close to the leading edge (LE). The separation region finally spread in the spanwise direction and contaminated a large part of the blade, whereas the boundary layer, in the very close region of the blade tip, was kept attached during the whole dynamic stall cycle because of tip vortex induced flow. Based on these previous studies, this paper proposes to go further into the complexity of the flow configuration and to investigate a helicopter rotor flow in dynamic stall condition. The first experimental analysis of the characteristics of the dynamic stall on helicopter environment was done by Bousman, based on UH-60A flight tests (Ref. 17). Bousman specially identified multiple dynamic stall events on different radial and azimuthal stations of the rotor disk. Numerical simulations of dynamic stall on rotor configurations were investigated, first in 2006, by Potsdam et al. for a UH-60A flight-test configuration (Ref. 18), and more recently by Ortun et al. (Ref. 19) and Yeo et al. (Ref. 20) for the wind tunnel scaled 7A rotor. In these two last references, the simulations were performed by means of a loose coupling method between a CFD code and a comprehensive analysis (CA) code. U.S. Army used Helios (Ref. 21) for CFD and RCAS (Ref. 22) for CA, whereas ONERA used elsA (Ref. 23) for CFD and HOST (Ref. 24) for CA. Both approaches have showed their capabilities to accurately predict and understand structural loads of the 7A rotor in two flight conditions including dynamic stall. However, a detailed analysis of the flow separation mechanisms during dynamic stall is still missing. The objective of the present paper is to bring some new elements of understanding concerning the stall mechanisms on rotor environment by means of suitable postprocessing of numerical simulation.
After a brief introduction of the flow configuration and the numerical methods, the results of the numerical simulation are validated in the second section by comparison with the available experimental data. Then, a thorough analysis of the numerical results is proposed in the last section, to identify and characterize the mechanisms of flow separation and propose a scenario of dynamic stall in this rotor environment.
Flow Configuration and Numerical Methods
The flow configuration is taken from the 7A rotor data base, tested in the S1MA wind tunnel of ONERA, in Modane (France) in 1991 (Refs. 25 and 26). This rotor of radius R = 2.1 m is composed of four articulated blades of constant chord c = 0.14 m. For the inboard part of the blade (r/R ≤ 0.75) a 13% relative thickness OA213 airfoil is used, whereas the 9% relative thickness OA209 airfoil is used in the tip region for r/R ≥ 0.9. The aerodynamic twist of the blade follows a linear law with a constant rate of −8.3 deg/R. The ONERA S1MA is a transonic closed circuit atmospheric wind tunnel. The test section has a diameter of 8 m. The experimental database provides unsteady pressure measurements, integrated section normal forces, and pitching moments at the five blade radial stations r/R = 0.5, 0.7, 0.825, 0.915, and 0.975. Dynamic stall has been experimentally observed on the 7A rotor for a forward flight condition at a moderate advance ratio μ = 0.3 and a high lift coefficient CL/σ = 0.1. This configuration is retained for the numerical investigation presented hereafter. The propulsive force coefficient is CX/σ = 0.0045, and the rotor is trimmed to satisfy the so-called Modane flapping law: β1S = 0 and β1C = −θ1S . All the flow parameters are summed up in Table 1. The computations are performed with the elsA solver, which discretizes the URANS equations on structured multiblock grids with a finite-volume approach (Ref. 23). A Chimera technique is adopted, with near body curvilinear grids around each blade and a multiresolution background Cartesian grid. The body meshes have an O-grid topology in the chord and spanwise directions and extend over one chord from the blade wall (Fig. 1(a)). The Cartesian grid is made of several near matching blocks with different levels of resolution (Fig. 1(b)). The Cartesian blocks closest to the body grids have a grid size length of 10% of chord (Fig. 1(c)). The test-stand is not taken into account in this simulation. The computational domain extends from −25R to 25R in the three directions around the rotor. Since Ortun et al. showed that the wind tunnel walls have no effect on the blade airloads (Ref. 19), these are not modeled in the simulation and far-field boundary conditions are imposed in the external edges of the computational domain. The spatial resolution characteristics of the simulation are shown in Table 2. Each blade mesh is composed of 5.7 million points. The total number of points including the body grids of the four blades and the background Cartesian grid is 43.6 million points. The near-wall grid resolution on the blade has been made to be as close as possible as the requirements proposed in Ref. 13 for the configuration of an oscillating wing. In the chordwise direction, x/c reaches 0.11% at the LE and 1.6% at midchord, where Costes et al. recommend 0.05% and 0.45% in Ref. 13. The spanwise resolution
