Analysis
Identification of different stall regions
It is proposed here to extract, from the CFD results, the separation points for each blade radial station and at each time step, and finally draw a rotor map of the separation point to exhibit the existence, the localization, and the dynamics of the flow separation event(s) that occurred on the rotor blades during the simulation. To do that, a separation criterion is needed. Most of the literature dedicated to this problem has agreed that a suitable criterion to define attached and separated boundary layers should be related to the shape factor Hi, which is defined as the ratio of the momentum over displacement boundary layer thicknesses. In 2004, Castillo et al. proposed an expression of the critical shape factor at the separation point as Hsep = 2.76 ± 0.23 (Ref. 36). This criterion provides satisfactory agreement with the experimental data of Refs. 37 and 38. Based on the CFD results of the present helicopter rotor simulation, the chordwise evolution of the shape factor with respect to the azimuthal position has been computed and is represented in Fig. 4 at four radial stations on the retreating side of the rotor disk. To ease the reading of the figures, the shape factor is only shown in a range between 0 and 3. The attached boundary layer is characterized by a low level of Hi, whereas the boundary layer is expected to be separated for the highest values. Figure 4 thus provides a qualitative view of the motion of the separation point along the chord as a function of the azimuthal position. Based on the conclusion of Ref. 36, the critical value Hi = 2.7 is shown in the dashed line as an indicator of the flow separation. In the innermost section (r/R = 0.5), the flow starts to separate at ψ = 180 deg from the trailing edge (TE) region (Fig. 4(a)). As ψ increases, the separation point moves progressively from the trailing to the LE as indicated by the black arrow in the figure. At ψ = 250 deg, the shape factor distribution indicates that the flow separation is expected to cover the entire blade
chord. Then, from 250 to 290 deg of azimuth, the separation point moves progressively back to the TE. At r/R = 0.7 (Fig. 4(b)), the evolution of the shape factor is similar, except that the separation does not reach the LE but the midchord. In Fig. 4(c), the behavior is completely different. On this section (r/R = 0.915), high values of Hi suddenly appear at the LE for ψ = 270 deg and then rapidly spread to the TE as ψ increases. This motion of the separation point is illustrated by a black arrow on the right-hand side of the figure. A second event appears later, between ψ = 330 and 360 deg. It is pointed out by a second black arrow on the left-hand side of Fig. 4(c). In this event, the separation also starts from the LE region. But contrary to the previous separation event, the high values of Hi stays confined in a small region close to the LE without any spreading in the chordwise direction. This signifies that the flow reattaches to the wall before the TE, giving rise to a separation bubble. A subsequent analysis of the skin friction lines will confirm the observations based on the shape factor. On the tip section (r/R = 0.975), Fig. 4(d) exhibits a first separated flow region between ψ = 300 and 320 deg. Every chordwise position undergoes a growth of the shape factor at the same azimuthal angle, which suggests that this results from the radial contamination of the first flow separation that occurred previously on section r/R = 0.915. Figure 4(d) also exhibits a second flow separation event at the end of the fourth quadrant of the rotor disk and which is confined to the LE region and similar to what is observed for section r/R = 0.915 in Fig. 4(c). To have an overall view of the dynamics of the flow separation(s) and to confirm the observations made with Fig. 4, the separation point expressed in the blade element local frame and noted xsep has been computed along the blades for each time step using a criterion based on the shape factor. Following the range of the critical shape factor proposed by Castillo et al. in Ref. 36, separation point location is here defined as the chordwise x coordinate where the shape factor gets higher than the critical value Hsep = 2.7. This provides the rotor map of Fig. 5. The separation is expressed in the local frame and made nondimensional with respect to the chord, so that xsep/c = 0 when the flow separation occurs at the LE and xsep/c = 1 when the flow separation occurs at the TE, which means that the boundary layer remains attached all along the chord. Three distinct regions have been pointed out in Fig. 5, each one
