Stall onset and blade–vortex interaction
The role of blade tip vortices in the dynamic stall process is investigated in this last part of the paper. Figure 10 shows an isocontour of Q-criterion for four azimuthal positions of the retreating blade: ψ = 255 deg, ψ = 270 deg, ψ = 300 deg, and ψ = 315 deg. The isocontour of Q-criterion reveals two different sources of vorticity inside the flow. The first one concerns the vortices that are generated at the blade tip by a primarily inviscid process. Thus, this kind of vortex is characterized by a low level of turbulent-to-laminar viscosity ratio μt /μ as shown in light gray in Fig. 10. A second kind of vorticity also emerges inside the flow. This one is due to the vortex shedding resulting from stall. In this case, the vortex contains high levels of μt /μ, since the vorticity is generated by the separation of the turbulent viscous boundary layer. These stall vortices appear in dark gray in Fig. 10. At the azimuths ψ = 255 and 270 deg, TE stall occurs on the blade radial stations 0.4 ≤ r/R ≤ 0.8. Figures 10(a) and 10(b) reveal that this stall event starts on the blade part that undergoes the impact of the tip vortex emitted by the previous blade. At these azimuthal stations, the blade–vortex interaction (BVI) is oblique. Flow separation occurs on the outboard part of the tip vortex impact where the local angle of attack is increased because of the vertical upwash velocity induced by the tip vortex. The stall vortex is thus emitted on the outboard part of the BVI region, which means that the stall vortex appears on the bottom left-hand side of the tip vortex in Figs. 10(a) and 10(b). The azimuthal angles ψ = 300 and 315 deg are related to the TE stall that occurs on the outboard part of the blade (r/R ≥ 0.9). Figures 10(c) and 10(d) show that this stall event also coincides with the impact of the tip vortex coming from the previous blade. The difference with the previous stall event relies on the fact that the BVI is here almost parallel and not oblique. Thus, the stall vortex is emitted after the BVI, which corresponds to the right-hand side of the tip vortex in Figs. 10(c) and 10(d). Finally, the relative positions of the tip and stall vortices are opposite to each other between the TE stall region (inboard part of blade) and the LE stall region (outboard part of the blade). In between, the blade tip and stall vortices merge with each other in the region indicated in Fig. 10(c). This vortex-merging process distorts the classical helical shape of the blade tip vortex and gives rise to a vortex tangle, which is still visible in the rotor wake as shown in Fig. 10(d). The TE and LE stall events that occur for this flight condition are thus undoubtedly triggered by a BVI phenomenon. This kind of scenario of dynamic stall in helicopter rotor environment is unusual in the literature. The classical description of dynamic stall, as it is generally assumed to occur on a helicopter rotor, is primarily due to the pitching and flapping motion, which makes the retreating blade reach such high angle of attack that dynamic stall occurs (Refs. 40–42). This idea led the helicopter community to investigate simplified configurations of oscillating pitching airfoils in uniform inflow which are indeed sufficient to reproduce the features of this kind of rotor stall scenario. BVI is also often neglected in the literature even when dynamic stall is addressed on rotating blades (Ref. 43), since this is largely accepted to have a minor effect. However, Gibertini and colleagues recently investigated experimentally the effect of BVI on an oscillating pitching airfoil, pointing out that BVI could trigger locally the stall onset on helicopter rotor blades (Refs. 44 and 45). The numerical investigation performed here proves that the impact of the tip vortex emitted by the previous blade indeed causes the beginning of
