stall. The bounded part of blade affected by this BVI thus undergoes a very rapid local increase of angle of attack which is added to its value, already high because of the pitching and flapping motion of the blade. This part of blade reaches higher angle of attack than its neighbors do. Thus, the stall onset is observed exactly where the tip vortex impacts the blade. This occurs first in the inboard part of the blade where the interaction is oblique. As the blade moves in azimuth, the position of the BVI moves from the inboard to the outboard parts of the blade. The stall onset along the blade radius then follows the motion of the BVI region, from the inboard to the outboard blade sections. This exactly corresponds to the radial motion of the separation point illustrated in Fig. 5 in the TE stall region. At the end of the BVI process, the tip vortex impact is no longer oblique but parallel to the blade, now causing the second stall referred to as the LE stall in Fig. 5 since the flow here separates abruptly from the LE. The role of BVI in the stall onset is thus clearly highlighted for this particular rotor configuration. However, it does not prove that dynamic stall always occurs this way for every kind of helicopter rotor flight conditions. The stations on the rotor disk where BVI can occur and the intensity of this interaction depend on many parameters of the rotor flow: thrust coefficient, rotor shaft angle, advance ratio, etc. The wind tunnel test flow parameters presented in Table 1 are meant to be representative of a helicopter rotor in high-thrust forward flight at moderate advance ratio. However, if, for instance, a higher propulsive coefficient had been chosen, the rotor shaft angle would have been higher and the tip vortices would have been convected further downstream, away from the retreating
blade, so that the BVI intensity would have been significantly reduced or even removed. Thus, it is not obvious whether, in this condition, stall would have occurred, and whether the impact of the tip vortices would have triggered the stall onset. Further numerical investigations will thus be necessary to clarify the role of BVI in the rotor stall process as a function of the rotor flow conditions.
Conclusions
The dynamic stall of the isolated 7A rotor in moderate-speed and highthrust forward flight has been numerically investigated. A weak coupling strategy between ONERA CFD code elsA and Airbus Helicopters CA code HOST has been employed. Attention has been paid to the effect of time and space resolution on the quality of the numerical results, by basing this analysis on the requirements of previous numerical investigations of stall phenomena for simpler configurations of two-dimensional airfoils and three-dimensional finite span wings. The numerical simulation has provided very satisfactory results by comparison with the experimental data in terms of section normal forces and pitching moments. A thorough analysis of the flow separation has shown the existence of three different separated flow regions on the rotor disk. The first one concerns the inboard part of the blade, occurs in the third quadrant of the rotor disk, and is of TE stall type. The second one occurs close to the blade tip at the beginning of the fourth quadrant and is of LE stall type. In the third region, located on the blade tip at the end of the fourth quadrant, a small separation bubble appears at the root of a shock wave, but cannot be
